{"name":"Bazooka Pink","hex":"#ffa6c9","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bazooka","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A primitive trombone having wide tubes.","A shoulder-held rocket launcher used as an antitank weapon, developed by America during World War II and so-called from its resemblance to the bazooka musical instrument."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To shoot with a bazooka."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bazooka"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bazooka","blocks":[{"text":"The bazooka is a man-portable recoilless anti-tank rocket launcher weapon, widely deployed by the United States Army, especially during World War II. Also referred to as the \"stovepipe\", the innovative bazooka was among the first generation of rocket-propelled anti-tank weapons used in infantry combat. Featuring a solid-propellant rocket for propulsion, it allowed for high-explosive anti-tank (HEAT) shaped charge warheads to be delivered against armored vehicles, machine gun nests, and fortified bunkers at ranges beyond that of a standard thrown grenade or mine. The universally applied nickname arose from the weapon's M1 variant's vague resemblance to the musical instrument called a bazooka invented and popularized by 1930s American comedian Bob Burns."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Soldier_with_Bazooka_M1.jpg/330px-Soldier_with_Bazooka_M1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bazooka","wikidata":"Q319432"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Bell_telephone_magazine_%281946%29_%2814569350208%29.jpg/500px-Bell_telephone_magazine_%281946%29_%2814569350208%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Bell_telephone_magazine_%281946%29_%2814569350208%29.jpg/1280px-Bell_telephone_magazine_%281946%29_%2814569350208%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Bell_telephone_magazine_%281946%29_%2814569350208%29.jpg/500px-Bell_telephone_magazine_%281946%29_%2814569350208%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A prototype of the world's first \"tube-type rocket-propelled grenade launcher\", the grip part of the \"Original Bazooka\" is marked \"May 1942\" and is signed by the development team including Hickman, Skinner, and Uhl","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABell_telephone_magazine_(1946)_(14569350208).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Bazookasmithsonian.jpg/500px-Bazookasmithsonian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Bazookasmithsonian.jpg/500px-Bazookasmithsonian.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An M1 Bazooka with M6A1 and M6A3 rockets","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABazookasmithsonian.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/American_soldier_with_M1A1_Bazooka_on_23_August_1944_near_Fontainebleau%2C_France%2C_from-_American_Troops_near_Fontainebleau_NARA_111-SC-193562cropped_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/American_soldier_with_M1A1_Bazooka_on_23_August_1944_near_Fontainebleau%2C_France%2C_from-_American_Troops_near_Fontainebleau_NARA_111-SC-193562cropped_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"American soldier with an M1A1 Bazooka on August 23, 1944, near Fontainebleau, France","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmerican_soldier_with_M1A1_Bazooka_on_23_August_1944_near_Fontainebleau%2C_France%2C_from-_American_Troops_near_Fontainebleau_NARA_111-SC-193562cropped_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Luccaitaly1944.png/500px-Luccaitaly1944.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Luccaitaly1944.png/500px-Luccaitaly1944.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A U.S. soldier fires an M9 Bazooka at a German machine gun nest, Lucca 1944","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALuccaitaly1944.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-708-0299-06%2C_Russland-S%C3%BCd%2C_Soldaten%2C_Sturmgesch%C3%BCtz_III.jpg/500px-Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-708-0299-06%2C_Russland-S%C3%BCd%2C_Soldaten%2C_Sturmgesch%C3%BCtz_III.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-708-0299-06%2C